syntax:
The core syntax involves the `typeid` operator:

```c++
#include <typeinfo>
#include <iostream>

class Base { virtual void f() {} };
class Derived : public Base {};

int main() {
  Base* b = new Derived();
  std::cout << typeid(*b).name() << std::endl; // Prints the name of the derived class at runtime (e.g., 'Derived' or similar depending on compiler)
  std::cout << typeid(b).name() << std::endl; // Prints the name of the base class pointer type
  delete b;
  return 0;
}
```

The `typeid` operator returns a `std::type_info` object that can be compared for equality or have its name retrieved using `name()`. Note that the exact output of `name()` is implementation-defined and not guaranteed to be human-readable.  Moreover, for polymorphic classes, virtual function tables are involved, making the runtime cost more significant than for non-polymorphic classes. This also explains why the second `typeid` call returns `Base*` instead of `Derived*`.
